## What happens

`0.0.1` is written out by hand in eight places, and one of them is a *parser*:
`stop` only recognises a pidfile header whose version is exactly `0.0.1`.

```
$ grep -rn '0\.0\.1' --include=*.rs src/      # doctored to show only the version uses
src/cli.rs:141:    "psgen 0.0.1 - realistic Linux process lists, …
src/cli.rs:765:        sys::out(b"psgen 0.0.1\n");
src/cli.rs:781:            sys::out(b"psgen 0.0.1\n");
src/doctor.rs:165:        out.push_str("\"version\":\"0.0.1\",\"ok\":");
src/doctor.rs:222:        out.push_str("psgen 0.0.1 doctor\n");
src/inject.rs:572:    buf.push_str("# psgen 0.0.1 boot ");
src/inject.rs:1149:        if let Some(rest) = line.strip_prefix(b"# psgen 0.0.1 boot ") {
```

Also `.github/release-notes.md:1` and `README.md:40,148`, and
`tests/vm.sh:65` greps for the exact string.

## Why it matters beyond tidiness

`src/inject.rs:1149` is the only place the boot-id guard is armed:

```rust
if let Some(rest) = line.strip_prefix(b"# psgen 0.0.1 boot ") {
    let boot = str::trim(rest).to_vec();
    …
}
if let Some(hb) = &header_boot {
    let cur = util::boot_id();
    if !force && !hb.is_empty() && !cur.is_empty() && *hb != cur {
        sys::err(b"psgen: pidfile is from another boot; use --force to ignore\n");
```

Bump the version to `0.0.2` and the `strip_prefix` silently stops matching, so a
pidfile from before a reboot is no longer detected as stale: `stop` will happily
check identities and signal whatever now owns those PIDs. Failing open is the
wrong direction for the one check that exists to prevent exactly that. The same
`strip_prefix` also breaks `stop` on a pidfile written by an older binary kept
around after an upgrade.

## Patch (verified)

One source of truth for the string:

```diff
--- a/src/cli.rs
+++ b/src/cli.rs
-fn usage() -> &'static str {
-    "psgen 0.0.1 - realistic Linux process lists, on stdout or in the real process table
+const VERSION_LINE: &[u8] = concat!("psgen ", env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"), "\n").as_bytes();
+
+fn usage() -> Vec<u8> {
+    let mut out = Buf::new();
+    out.push_str(concat!(
+        "psgen ",
+        env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"),
+        " - realistic Linux process lists, on stdout or in the real process table"));
+    out.push_str(USAGE_BODY);
+    out.v
+}
+
+const USAGE_BODY: &str = "
 …
-"
-}
+";
@@
-            out.push_str(usage());
+            out.push_bytes(&usage());
@@
-        sys::out(usage().as_bytes());
+        sys::out(&usage());
@@
-        sys::out(b"psgen 0.0.1\n");
+        sys::out(VERSION_LINE);
```

`doctor.rs`: `concat!("psgen ", env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"), " doctor\n")` and
`concat!("\"version\":\"", env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"), "\",\"ok\":")`.

`inject.rs`, writer:

```rust
buf.push_str("# psgen ");
buf.push_str(env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"));
buf.push_str(" boot ");
buf.push_bytes(&boot);
```

`inject.rs`, reader — parse the header without the version in it, so an upgrade
cannot blind the boot-id check:

```rust
/// The boot id from a `# psgen <version> boot <boot-id>` line.
///
/// The version is not interpreted, so a pidfile written by a different
/// release is still recognised after an upgrade.
fn pidfile_boot_id(line: &[u8]) -> Option<Vec<u8>> {
    let rest = line.strip_prefix(b"# psgen ")?;
    let at = str::find(rest, b" boot ")?;
    Some(str::trim(&rest[at + 6..]).to_vec())
}
…
-        if let Some(rest) = line.strip_prefix(b"# psgen 0.0.1 boot ") {
-            let boot = str::trim(rest).to_vec();
+        if let Some(boot) = pidfile_boot_id(line) {
```

Verified: `--version`, `--help`, `doctor`, `doctor --json` and the pidfile header
all print `0.0.1` read from `Cargo.toml`; a hand-written
`# psgen 9.9.9 boot deadbeef` pidfile is now recognised and refused
(`pidfile is from another boot`) instead of being parsed as "no header", and the
repository suite still passes.

`tests/vm.sh:65` should then grep `'^psgen 0\.'` or the version from
`Cargo.toml` rather than the literal.

## Environment

psgen 0.0.1-beta.1, source at `bf80156`. Pure source change, no runtime
dependency on the host.
